outputs:
Answer: B

Rationale: In the mid-pelvis (often referred to as the plane of least dimensions), both the transverse and anteroposterior (AP) diameters are essentially the same—approximately 10.5 cm to 11 cm. This makes the mid-pelvic plane roughly circular.  
• Option A (“The normal transverse diameter of the pelvic mid-cavity is 11 cm”) is close to typical textbook values (10.5–11 cm) but by itself does not distinguish it from the AP diameter;  
• Option C (13.5 cm for the transverse outlet) is too large (the transverse outlet is usually about 11 cm);  
• Option D incorrectly states a 3 cm difference between the true and diagonal conjugates (the difference is usually about 1.5–2 cm);  
• Option E incorrectly defines the AP diameter of the outlet (it is measured from the inferior margin of the pubic symphysis to the tip of the sacrum or sacrococcygeal joint, not the lower border of the coccyx).

